\DOC LOCAL_SUB_P521_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SUB_P521_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic-execution goal over an inlined modular-subtraction
subroutine, replacing the current machine state with a successor state and
adding an assumption characterising the stored result.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} (zero in all observed traces); the second is a
{string list} of three names giving the result variable followed by the two
operand variables of the subtraction whose specification is introduced. The
goal is an {eventually} statement about a state variable, and the tactic yields
a single subgoal in which that state variable is replaced throughout the
conclusion by a fresh successor state. Every assumption mentioning the old
state — the program-counter value, {bytes_loaded}, register and memory-content
readings, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame relating the initial state to
the current one — is discharged and re-added in a form about the new state,
with the program counter advanced and the {MAYCHANGE} frame extended by the
memory block holding the result; the memory-content assumption for the
destination block now equates it with the named result variable. A new
implication assumption is also introduced, conditional on the two named
operands being below the field modulus and stating that the result is their
modular difference. Purely arithmetic and non-overlapping assumptions are left
untouched, though their positions in the assumption list may shift.
{
      A ?- t
      ======================================  LOCAL_SUB_P521_TAC n ["res"; "op1"; "op2"]
      A' ?- t'
}
\noindent Here {A'} is {A} with the state-dependent assumptions restated for
the successor state plus the new specification assumption, and {t'} is {t} with
the state variable replaced.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-subgoal transformation, always on
goals of this shape with a three-name argument list.

\ENDDOC
